  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 248 AM EST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nA fairly complex set up going into the long term, which will be \npartially dependent on the evolution of the currently developing \nsystem. The short term dominate system (that which is developing \nright now off the FL/GA coast), may bring both cold air (mid 30s) \nand some limited wrap around moisture to far north GA. No \nsignificant impacts are expected from this, however folks in the \nmountains may wake up to some frosting or even a few flurries on \nFriday.\n\nThe complex interaction between this system has potential to impact \nflow upstream. Models remain fairly consistent on the development of \na cutoff lobe of energy from the developing trough off the west \ncoast. However, models struggle to resolve features like this \ndynamically and temporally, especially when they extend down towards \nthe US/Mexico boarder. Model spread on the development and timing \nremains high at this time. Conditions between the two systems will \nbe drier and cold as the stalling low interaction from the first \nsystem is caught by the secondary polar wave. Reinforced dry cold \nair from the plains will keeps lows in the 30s and low 40s Saturday \nand Sunday. Further temperature forecasting will be highly dependent \non the strength and forward speed of the cutoff low. Once the cut-\noff low energy makes it to our area (either as a closed low or an \nopen wave), warmer air from the south should increase daily highs at \nleast into the upper 50s and maybe into the 60s mid next week. Rain \nchances also increase early next week, however this is even more \ndependent on the strength of the parent low and how entrenched cold \nair may be from the exiting high.",
